<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: Al-Andalus</h1><p class="ws-lede">Nearly eight centuries of Muslim Iberia, from Tariq's crossing in 711 to the surrender of Granada in 1492.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/al-andalus">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the stops from 1 (earliest) to 6 (latest), by when each began: a state&#8217;s founding, a person&#8217;s birth, the date of an event.</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Caliphate of Córdoba</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Emirate of Córdoba</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Umayyad conquest of Hispania</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Averroes</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Maimonides</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Key terms</h2><p class="ws-how">The path uses these words. Explain each one in your own words.</p><ul class="ws-terms"><li><b>Arabic language</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Latin</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Umayyad conquest of Hispania</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;In 711 Tariq ibn Ziyad crossed from North Africa and defeated King Roderic; within a few years most of the peninsula was under Umayyad rule.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 5, <b>Caliphate of Córdoba</b>: &#8220;Abd al-Rahman III's caliphate of 929 made Córdoba the largest city of western Europe, with the palace city of Madinat al-Zahra and celebrated libraries.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 9, <b>Emirate of Granada</b>: &#8220;The last Muslim state in Iberia survived as a tributary of Castile for over 250 years, built the Alhambra and surrendered on 2 January 1492.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;Nearly eight centuries of Muslim Iberia, from Tariq's crossing in 711 to the surrender of Granada in 1492.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/al-andalus. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: Al-Andalus</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Umayyad conquest of Hispania <span>711 CE</span></li><li>Emirate of Córdoba <span>from 756 CE</span></li><li>Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba <span>785</span></li><li>Caliphate of Córdoba <span>from 929 CE</span></li><li>Averroes <span>born 1126 CE</span></li><li>Maimonides <span>born c. 1138 CE</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Key terms</h2><dl class="ws-key-terms"><dt>Arabic language</dt><dd>Arabic is a Semitic language that spread with Islam from the Arabian Peninsula across the Middle East and North Africa.</dd><dt>Latin</dt><dd>Latin is the language of ancient Rome, which spread across the western Mediterranean with Roman rule and became the root of the Romance languages.</dd></dl></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
